s of his miserly life. These ghostly visits include encounters with the Ghost of Christmas Past, who begins Scrooge's journey of self-reflection by reflecting on past Christmases, his experiences with family, and the joyful times at Fezziwig's warehouse. The Ghost of Christmas Present shows him various scenes to reflect upon his life and actions, including joyful Christmas scenes with a family, and inquires about Tiny Tim's future health, teaching him lessons from his experiences. The Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come offers a more somber reflection, showing him the consequences of his actions, the nature of death, and contemplating a covered body, which deeply affects Scrooge.\n\nThroughout these supernatural events, Scrooge is depicted as a man experiencing reluctance, fear, and curiosity about the unfolding events. He is tormented by spirits showing him past, present, and future events to reflect on his life choices and attitudes towards wealth and happiness. His own words and actions are used against him to show him the error of his ways. Despite his initial skepticism and preference for darkness, indicating his miserly nature, Scrooge undergoes a profound experience of redemption over the course of one evening. He becomes a changed man, showing regret for his past and fear for his future, which leads to a transformation in his character.\n\nBy the end of the narrative, Scrooge is depicted as a man who has changed his outlook on life, vowing to honor Christmas in his heart and keep it all year. He becomes generous, raises Bob Cratch\",88\r\n30,MARLEY,\"Marley, who has been deceased for seven years, is vividly remembered and referenced throughout the narrative as Scrooge's former business partner. His presence haunts Scrooge in various forms, notably through the imagery of a locomotive hearse, the tiles in the fireplace, and most strikingly as a ghostly apparition on Scrooge's door knocker. Marley's face and essence seem to linger around Scrooge, serving as a consta